<h1>Vocab: World Bus, Gossip, Journal</h1>
<dl>
<dt>world bus</dt>
<dd>The single shared store (<code>WorldState</code>) systems read facts
from instead of keeping private copies. The clock was the first tenant;
gossip facts the second; the journal reads it without new wiring.</dd>

<dt>injected time</dt>
<dd>Passing the shared clock into consumers as a parameter
(<code>update(..., timeOfDayHours)</code>) rather than letting them own a
timer — testable by varying the argument.</dd>

<dt>propose → validate → commit</dt>
<dd>The discipline for LLM-created state: the model proposes structured
candidates; code validates all-or-nothing; only validated records write.
Model output never touches the store directly.</dd>

<dt>knowledge set</dt>
<dd>Per-agent record of WHICH facts an agent has heard, stored beside the
facts (not inside them). Propagation and the journal's visibility filter
are both just queries over these sets.</dd>

<dt>propagation gates</dt>
<dd>The conservative conditions for spread: proximity radius, minimum
teller-knowledge age, per-tick chance, one fact per pair. Slower is
recoverable; instant town-wide knowledge is not.</dd>

<dt>read model</dt>
<dd>A derived, filtered view over shared state for one consumer — the
journal is a read model of the fact store (player-heard facts, grouped,
conflict-flagged) that owns no data and never writes.</dd>

<dt>corroboration vs conflict</dt>
<dd>Same subject + same content from two tellers = corroboration (not
flagged). Same subject + different content = conflict (both rows
flagged). Exact normalized subjects make the comparison honest.</dd>
</dl>
